{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Elohim called the rakia 'shamayim' (heavens).</b> On the second day He called them by this name when He clothed them with the form of rakia, for on the first day the heavens existed in the act of creation (beriah), but the name did not apply to them until they assumed this form. The meaning of this name is as though it were pointed with a segol under the shin — like the word (Song of Songs 1:7) שַׁלָּמָה אֶהְיֶה כְּעֹטְיָה — as though to say: shamayim is that which was hammered out (נרקע) and stretched like a tent within the upper and lower waters. Through this name He made known the secret of their formation. And in the Gemara in tractate Ḥagigah (Ḥagigah 12a) they said: What is שָׁמַיִם? שֵׁם מַיִם (the name of the waters). If so, one mem is elided for the conjunction of two identical letters, like the word יְרֻבַּעַל (Judges 6:32 and elsewhere), and it reads שֵׁם מַיִם, meaning: a name He called to the waters when He clothed them in another form. This is the plain sense of the verse according to the approach that Rashi wrote, and it is the opinion of Rav that we mentioned (Ramban on Genesis 1:6). The name 'the heavens' and 'the earth' in the first verse would then refer to what would later be called by those names, for it is not possible to designate them except in this language. But it is more correct, according to the plain sense of Scripture, to say that the heavens mentioned in the first verse are the supernal heavens; they are not inclusive of the spheres but are above the Chariot, as it says: And the likeness over the heads of the living creature was a rakia, like the color of awesome ice, stretched over their heads from above (Ezekiel 1:22). From them the Holy One, blessed be He, is called 'rider of the heavens' (Deuteronomy 33:26). Scripture did not recount anything about their creation, just as it made no mention of the angels, the living creatures of the Chariot, and every separate entity that is not corporeal. It did, however, mention regarding the heavens that they are created, meaning that their prior existence is nil. And on the second day He said that there shall be a rakia within the waters, meaning that from the waters previously mentioned — whose creation was noted — there would come into being a beaten-out expanse separating between them. He also called those spheres 'shamayim,' like the name of the first supernal heavens; therefore He calls them later in the parashah (Genesis 1:17) 'rakia of shamayim' — 'and Elohim set them in the rakia of the shamayim' — to clarify that these are not the heavens mentioned by the name shamayim but rather the raki'ot that He called shamayim. This too is the opinion of our Sages, which they mentioned in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 4:2); they said: All the rabbis say it in the name of Rabbi Ḥananya bar Rabbi Pinḥas and Rabbi Ya'akov bar Rabbi Avin in the name of Rabbi Shmuel bar Rabbi Naḥman: At the hour the Holy One, blessed be He, said 'Let there be a rakia within the waters,' the middle drop congealed and the lower heavens and the heavens of the supernal heavens were made. And this statement is expounded regarding the spheres of the galgalim, in which there are lower and upper ones called 'the heavens of the heavens,' as it is written (Psalms 148:3–4): Praise Him, sun and moon; praise Him, all stars of light; praise Him, heavens of the heavens and the waters that are above the heavens. But the heavens mentioned at the very beginning — where His throne is, as it is written (Isaiah 66:1), 'The heavens are My throne' — these are the ones mentioned at the beginning of this psalm (Psalms 148:1–2): Praise YHWH from the heavens; praise Him in the heights; praise Him, all His angels. And this language is correct in the plain sense of Scripture, though within the name 'heavens' and the name 'throne' there is also a supernal and hidden secret, for there are heavens of heavens and a throne of throne. From this the Sages say (Berakhot 13a): 'in order to accept upon himself the yoke of the kingdom of heaven,' and they said (ibid. 7a): 'fear of heaven,' and Scripture says (Daniel 4:23): 'Heaven rules,' and there is a wondrous midrash of theirs (Sefer ha-Bahir §100) on what is written: 'And You will hear the heavens' (1 Kings 8:32). All of this will be shown to be alluded to in the first verse to the one who merits it. Behold, the verses have clarified that the first-created entities came from nothingness, and the rest derive from the first created matter. And do not find difficult the statement of Rabbi Eliezer the Great (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer 3), who said: From where were the heavens created? From the light of the garment of the Holy One, blessed be He — and so it is also in Bereshit Rabbah — for because the Sages wished to elevate the first matter to its utmost limit and to make it finer than the finest, they did not consider that the heavens, which are a body in motion possessing matter and form, are the ones created from nothingness. Rather, the light of the garment is the first created entity, and from it issued the tangible matter in the heavens; and He gave the earth another matter, not as subtle as the first — namely, the snow that is beneath the Throne of Glory, for the Throne of Glory was created, and from it came the snow beneath it, and from it was formed the matter of the earth. And behold, it is third in the order of creation:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The commentator quotes the Sefer ha-Bahir by number reference (§100); the citation is rendered as found in the text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:11:46.955811775Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}